/** \file p4.dox
 * \brief User's Guide for Pixel Prediction Post-Processing and p4Reduce.
 * \author Jared R. Males
 */

/** \addtogroup p4reduce_user_guide
 * @{
 *
 * P4 (Pixel Prediction Post-Processing) predicts the time-variable stellar PSF and noise at one search pixel from a
 * local annular-wedge optimization region (OR). The complete time sequence trains one in-sample regression for each
 * search pixel. A central exclusion disk prevents the expected companion location from entering the predictor set.
 * `p4.regressionFrame` selects between the original detector-frame regression and a temporally centered regression
 * at fixed pixels in the derotated sky frame.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_detector_regression Detector-frame regression
 *
 * `p4.regressionFrame=detector` is the compatibility default and preserves the original P4 algorithm. For one fixed
 * detector search pixel, let `y` be its `T x 1` time series and let `X` contain `K` interpolated predictor-pixel time
 * series. P4 eigendecomposes the smaller of the two Gram matrices. For `T <= K` it forms the temporal Gram matrix
 *
 * \f[
 *     G_T = X X^T
 * \f]
 *
 * and subtracts each retained temporal eigenvector `u` as `u (u^T y)`. For `T > K` it forms the predictor Gram
 * matrix `G_K = X^T X` and `b = X^T y`, then applies the algebraically equivalent truncated pseudoinverse. Both
 * branches solve an `N x N` symmetric problem with `N = min(T,K)`, use the same nonzero eigenvalues and rank policy,
 * and add no hidden centering or intercept. Mean-image subtraction and other preprocessing are controlled entirely
 * by the inherited hciReduce preprocessing configuration.
 *
 * With the compatibility default `p4.numberImages=0`, each predictor row contains only the `K` OR pixels from its
 * central image. A positive `p4.numberImages=N` appends `N` nearest qualifying earlier-image blocks and `N` nearest
 * qualifying later-image blocks. The central block retains its `K_OR` OR pixels, while each neighbor contributes
 * only the `K_PSF` direct detector pixels inside `p4.psfRadius`, for `K_OR + 2N K_PSF` columns. At a cube endpoint,
 * missing neighbors on one temporal side are replaced by additional qualifying images from the available side,
 * preserving both predictor width and integration time. A neighbor qualifies when its wrapped field-rotation
 * displacement is at least `2 * p4.psfRadius` at that annulus's mean search radius, keeping the neighbor's physical
 * PSF disk free of companion signal. If the requested radius cannot retain a structurally valid PCA sample set, P4
 * uses the largest positive radius that does; if no positive radius works, that annulus gracefully reverts to
 * same-image predictors. This detector-only extension is rejected for `p4.regressionFrame=rotated`.
 *
 * The detector-frame residual cubes are derotated by the shared ADI final-processing stage before combination.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_local Pixel-local finite-amplitude trials
 *
 * `p4.localStampSize=W>0` evaluates one trial companion without processing the rest of the output field. It is an
 * exact finite-amplitude refit, not a frozen-coefficient response. For every detector search pixel needed by the
 * requested sky stamp and every temporal row, the configured trial perturbation `D_t` changes both sides of the
 * regression:
 *
 * \f[
 *   y'_p(t)=I_t(p)+D_t(p), \qquad
 *   X'_p(t,k)=S_{p,k}[I_t]+S_{p,k}[D_t].
 * \f]
 *
 * The ordinary P4 eigensolve is then repeated. Positive `p4.numberImages` is supported: direct physical-PSF-disk
 * predictors receive the perturbation from their selected physical frame. Every unique detector search pixel is fit
 * once with its complete configured time sequence, but only residual rows needed by the local derotation footprints
 * are retained. Cubic footprints are unioned over the complete field rotation, so the number of fitted detector
 * pixels is generally larger than `W*W` but much smaller than a complete output field.
 *
 * The source center is never quantized. Full images and the source template use the geometric center
 * `0.5*(N-1)` in each axis. The odd result lattice is merely anchored at the nearest integer pixel, while FITS
 * provenance records both that integer origin and the continuous source coordinate. When an odd `W` is used with
 * even detector/template dimensions, the internal centered template crop expands to `W+1`; cropping the template to
 * odd width would move its half-pixel center and is deliberately not done.
 *
 * The initial command-line path requires already-preprocessed inputs (`preProcess.skip=true`) and interprets the
 * existing single `[fake]` template as a perturbation at the P4-input stage. It supports detector regression,
 * derotation, and the ordinary mean, median, weighted-mean, or sigma-clipped final combination, while rejecting
 * rotated regression, post-median subtraction, per-frame PSF-subtracted output, and frozen-model PSF products. The
 * standard final-image name contains the local residual cube, and a neighboring `local_validity` FITS cube records
 * exact combined validity. See \ref p4_local_dictionary for the complete configuration and naming contract.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_frozen_psf Frozen-model PSF responses
 *
 * Setting `p4.psfFile` enables an analytic response calculation for the detector-frame, same-image-predictor
 * algorithm. Let `F_s(p)` denote the configured centered PSF template sampled at detector coordinate `p` for a unit
 * source centered at `s`, and let `S_k` be the exact cubic sampling operator for predictor `k`. For search coordinate
 * `q` and one supported truncated-PCA coefficient vector `beta_m`, the local signed response is
 *
 * \f[
 *   G_{q,m}(s) = F_s(q) - \sum_{k=1}^{K} \beta_{q,m,k}\, S_k[F_s].
 * \f]
 *
 * `beta_m` is the truncated minimum-norm pseudoinverse solution already used by the science regression. It is
 * accumulated while retained eigenmodes are available; no dense pseudoinverse is formed. The template calculation
 * and predictor sampling use float values, while coefficient formation retains P4's current double-precision PCA
 * arithmetic. Invalid and rank-unsupported fits have explicit zero validity and are never interpreted as a zero PSF.
 *
 * The response is `FROZEN_SIGNED`: it is the signed output of the fitted linear predictor with the coefficients held
 * fixed. The template's stored scale is preserved; no peak or integral normalization and no preprocessing are applied
 * to the template. The configured template must therefore represent the post-preprocessing P4 input stage. This is a
 * local filter template, not a calibrated throughput measurement: refitting after finite fake injection can change
 * the coefficients and is deliberately outside this linearized contract.
 *
 * Detector-local responses are retained at every owned search coordinate. Final-frame reconstruction maps each sky
 * stamp sample through the exact inverse derotation, composes the same cubic footprints used by science derotation
 * and local sampling, and combines its bounded frame stack with the configured science estimator, weights, sigma
 * threshold, minimum-good fraction, and response validity. Mean and weighted-mean responses are linear frozen-model
 * products. Median and actively sigma-clipped products are useful filter-template approximations evaluated from the
 * propagated response samples themselves; they are not finite-amplitude-independent photometric transfer functions.
 *
 * The first implementation intentionally rejects `p4.regressionFrame=rotated`, positive `p4.numberImages`, and
 * `adi.postMedSub=true` when a PSF template is configured. A complete final PSF field additionally requires a final
 * combination method; `combine.method=none` does not define one combined response.
 *
 * When `p4.outputPSFModels=true`, P4 writes a deterministic coordinate image, one response cube and one source-center
 * validity image per output mode, and a completion manifest under `p4.psfOutputPrefix` in the common output directory.
 * A response cube is `W x W x S`, where `W=p4.psfStampSize` and plane `s` maps to row `s` of the coordinate image.
 * Unsupported individual response samples are NaN. Model-only sets use schema version 1; enabling filtering uses
 * schema version 2 for the complete set. Every product records its role, template identity/stage/normalization,
 * `FROZEN_SIGNED`, combination policy, stamp and internal support geometry, storage estimates, source count, and mode
 * identity where applicable. Consumers must accept a set only when
 * `p4PSF_manifest.fits` (with the configured prefix substituted) contains both data value `1` and
 * `HIERARCH P4 PSF COMPLETE = 1`.
 *
 * With `p4.psfFilter=true`, the final signed response at output coordinate \f$q\f$ is applied directly to the
 * corresponding final science neighborhood without constructing a dense space-variant convolution matrix:
 *
 * \f[
 *   \widehat a(q) =
 *   \frac{\sum_{\delta \in V_q} H_q(\delta) I(q+\delta)}
 *        {\sum_{\delta \in V_q} H_q(\delta)^2}.
 * \f]
 *
 * Here \f$V_q\f$ contains response-valid samples whose science coordinates are in bounds and finite. The signed
 * numerator preserves negative subtraction lobes; the denominator normalizes the result to an amplitude relative to
 * the stored PSF-template scale. Filtering requires an odd stamp so every response sample maps to an integer science
 * pixel. A result is valid only when the denominator is positive and `p4.psfFilterMinGoodFract` does not exceed
 * \f$|V_q|/W^2\f$. Invalid amplitudes remain NaN rather than becoming zero.
 *
 * Filtering writes `finim_filtered_NNNN.fits`, `finim_filter_normalization_NNNN.fits`,
 * `finim_filter_support_NNNN.fits`, and `finim_filter_validity_NNNN.fits` for the default final-image prefix. The
 * products use the resolved ordinary final-image prefix and sequence number, and contain full-image cubes in ordinary
 * P4 mode order. With an exact final-image name, each role is inserted before its extension. These respectively
 * contain \f$\widehat a\f$, the retained \f$\sum H^2\f$ denominator, \f$|V_q|/W^2\f$, and exact zero/one validity.
 * Each product mirrors the complete ordinary final-image FITS header, then adds schema-2 cards recording the source
 * path, equation, response and template conventions, minimum support, mode mapping, and memory estimate.
 * `p4.outputPSFModels` may remain false; in that case no coordinate or compact model products are written. The
 * ordinary `finim` is unchanged in either case.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_rotated_regression Rotated-frame regression
 *
 * `p4.regressionFrame=rotated` learns a different coefficient vector at each fixed sky-frame search pixel. The OR
 * wedge is constructed once in that sky frame. For search pixel `m`, let \f$q_{m,0}=m\f$ be the target coordinate and
 * let \f$q_{m,k}\f$ be its `K` mapped radial/tangential predictor coordinates. For frame `t`, each fixed sky
 * coordinate is mapped directly back into the preprocessed, unrotated detector image using the configured derotation
 * angle:
 *
 * \f[
 * p_r = C_r+(q_r-C_r)\cos\theta_t+(q_c-C_c)\sin\theta_t,
 * \f]
 *
 * \f[
 * p_c = C_c-(q_r-C_r)\sin\theta_t+(q_c-C_c)\cos\theta_t.
 * \f]
 *
 * Here `C` is the geometric image center. The value
 * \f$u_{t,m}^{(k)}=x_t[p_{t,m,k}]\f$ is evaluated with one cubic-convolution interpolation in the preprocessed,
 * unrotated detector frame. No rotated image cube is materialized, and fractional OR coordinates are not
 * interpolated a second time.
 * The target `k=0` and every predictor use this same direct sampling operator.
 *
 * The target and each predictor column are then centered over the same `T` frames:
 *
 * \f[
 * \widetilde{u}_{t,m}^{(k)} = u_{t,m}^{(k)}-
 *     \frac{1}{T}\sum_{\tau=1}^{T}u_{\tau,m}^{(k)}.
 * \f]
 *
 * With `y_m(t)=\widetilde{u}_{t,m}^{(0)}` and `D_m(t,k)=\widetilde{u}_{t,m}^{(k)}`, each requested truncation minimizes
 *
 * \f[
 *     \left\|y_m-D_m c_m\right\|_2^2
 * \f]
 *
 * over its retained PCA subspace, using the same truncated eigensolution and rank policy as detector mode. Centering
 * removes one temporal degree of freedom, so mode fractions are realized against `min(K,T-1)`. At least two frames
 * are required, and the structural limit remains in force even when `p4.rankTolerance=0`.
 *
 * The centered matrices determine the truncated minimum-norm coefficient vector `c_m`, but that vector is applied to
 * the original uncentered predictor matrix `U_m`. The written residual is
 *
 * \f[
 *     r_m=u_m^{(0)}-U_m c_m.
 * \f]
 *
 * With \f$H=I-\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{T}/T\f$, `H r_m` minimizes the requested centered variance within the retained
 * PCA subspace, while the residual mean is
 * `mean(u_m^(0))-mean(U_m)c_m`. A constant added to the target does not change the fitted coefficients and passes
 * through to the residual, but a constant added to a predictor changes the applied prediction baseline. The baseline
 * convention is therefore the raw predictor level after inherited detector-frame preprocessing and direct rotated
 * sampling. A sky-stationary companion does not enter the centered target used to estimate the coefficients, but its
 * final throughput is not guaranteed solely by centering because the uncentered predictor model can change the
 * residual DC level.
 *
 * \warning Rotated regression is a numerical reference and has not passed scientific acceptance. On a 621-frame
 * AF Lep/NACO comparison, its radial-profile and detector-frame-mean-preprocessed products correlated above
 * 0.999997 with independently reconstructed target-only sky stacks; the P4 corrections had only about 0.2 percent
 * of the target-stack RMS. The corresponding detector-frame P4 control produced substantially lower residual noise
 * and higher companion SNR. The centered objective does not constrain the residual mean shown by a temporal
 * combination, and no replacement stellar-baseline rule has yet been selected. Use the default detector mode for
 * science reductions.
 *
 * Invalid or rank-unsupported locations are carried by an explicit per-output validity cube while arithmetic is in
 * progress. Rank-insufficient output pixels are invalid rather than silently clamped to a smaller mode count. In
 * detector mode validity is propagated through derotation and combination. Rotated residuals and validity are already
 * sky-aligned, so final processing combines them without applying a second derotation. Persistent invalid values are
 * materialized only after final arithmetic.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_modes Application modes
 *
 * `p4Reduce` supports `basic` and `normal` as exact aliases. Each performs one configured target-only ADI reduction.
 * Grid scheduling is deliberately external to the executable. Saved-product `postprocess` mode is not yet supported:
 * it requires a versioned raw-residual and validity-mask checkpoint format so invalid pixels cannot contaminate a
 * resumed derotation or combination.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_configuration P4 configuration
 *
 * The application reuses the ordinary hciReduce input, mask, fake-injection, preprocessing, coaddition, ADI,
 * combination, and output settings. P4 adds only these targets:
 *
 * | Target | Contract |
 * |---|---|
 * | `geom.minRadius`, `geom.maxRadius` | Matching, ordered, non-overlapping full-annulus bounds. Inner radii are inclusive and outer radii are exclusive. |
 * | `p4.regressionFrame` | `detector` (default) or `rotated`; selects the regression coordinates, centering rule, support test, and final-frame lifecycle. |
 * | `p4.numberImages` | Nonnegative count of qualifying earlier and later detector frames added to each predictor row; `0` is the compatibility default and positive values are invalid in rotated mode. |
 * | `p4.localStampSize` | Zero for a complete reduction, or a positive odd sky-stamp width for one finite-amplitude local refit using the `[fake]` settings. |
 * | `p4.modeFractions` | Finite, strictly increasing fractions in `(0,1]`. Each annulus realizes `floor(fraction * min(T,K))` in detector mode or `floor(fraction * min(T-1,K))` in rotated mode; zero or duplicate realized counts are rejected. |
 * | `p4.orDeltaRadiusInner`, `p4.orDeltaRadiusOuter` | Positive inward and outward radial extents of the local predictor wedge, in pixels. |
 * | `p4.orArcHalfWidth` | Nonnegative azimuthal half-width expressed as arc length in pixels; `0` uses only `p4.orMaxHalfAngle`. |
 * | `p4.orMaxHalfAngle` | Angular half-width cap in `(0,180]` degrees; `180` permits the full 360-degree annulus. |
 * | `p4.psfRadius` | Positive physical radius of the central signal-exclusion disk, in pixels. |
 * | `p4.psfFile` | Optional centered, finite PSF template at the post-preprocessing P4 input stage; enables detector-frame frozen-model calculation. |
 * | `p4.psfStampSize` | Positive square final response-stamp size required with `p4.psfFile`; odd and even sizes use geometric centers. |
 * | `p4.outputPSFModels` | Write the compact combined spatially variable PSF field; default `false`. |
 * | `p4.psfFilter` | Write the signed normalized spatially variable filter products without replacing the ordinary final image; default `false`. |
 * | `p4.psfFilterMinGoodFract` | Minimum usable fraction of the full local stamp in `[0,1]`; default `1`. |
 * | `p4.psfOutputPrefix` | Compact model/manifest prefix in the common output directory; default `p4PSF_`. |
 * | `p4.exclusionPolicy` | Required `sampleCenter` or `kernelSupport` policy. |
 * | `p4.exclusionRadiusBuffer` | Explicit nonnegative amount added to `p4.psfRadius`; `0.5` reproduces the prototype's effective mask buffer. |
 * | `p4.rankTolerance` | Relative eigenvalue threshold in `[0,1)`, default `1e-12`. |
 * | `p4.memoryFraction` | Fraction of Linux `MemAvailable` reserved for future P4 allocations, default `0.8`; `0` disables automatic worker limiting. |
 * | `p4.writeDiagnostics` | Enable ownership, validity, canonical-region, rank, and timing FITS diagnostics. Every diagnostic records `HIERARCH P4 FRAME`. |
 * | `p4.diagnosticDirectory` | Destination for enabled diagnostics. |
 *
 * P4 rejects configured RDI input, overlapping annuli, and nonfinite target input as hard errors. In detector mode the
 * geometry must fit its configured interpolation footprints. In rotated mode every target and predictor must have a
 * complete direct detector-frame cubic footprint in every input frame. If any one of those footprints crosses an edge
 * or touches a zero-valued common-mask pixel, that sky search pixel is invalid in every frame and mode plane; frames
 * are never silently dropped from a local fit.
 *
 * `sampleCenter` reproduces the prototype's center-only signal exclusion. In rotated mode `kernelSupport` checks the
 * exact detector-frame cubic footprints produced by the inverse mapping over all search pixels and frames, then removes
 * an offending predictor column from the annulus-wide OR. Thus every retained local fit has one common `K`, while
 * ordinary mask or edge failures invalidate only the affected search pixel.
 *
 * The rank test is the literal strict comparison `lambda > p4.rankTolerance * lambdaMax`. A zero tolerance therefore
 * accepts every eigenvalue returned as positive, including roundoff-positive numerical nulls, except that rotated
 * mode always enforces its structural `T-1` limit. Use a nonzero relative tolerance when reliable classification of
 * other rank deficiencies matters.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_memory Memory-bounded execution
 *
 * When a final combination is requested and `output.outputPSFSub=false`, P4 retains regression residuals only at the
 * owned search pixels. It materializes one full residual cube and one full validity cube at a time, runs the ordinary
 * post-median, derotation, and combination lifecycle for that output mode, retains the combined two-dimensional
 * plane, then releases the temporary cubes before processing the next mode. The final FITS plane order and numerical
 * values are unchanged. `combine.method=none` retains all full residual cubes because they are then the requested
 * result; `output.outputPSFSub=true` also uses the retained-cube path so every per-frame product remains available.
 *
 * P4 estimates private PCA workspace from each annulus's temporal sample count, predictor count, and requested output
 * count. With the default `p4.memoryFraction=0.8`, Linux `MemAvailable` supplies a future-allocation budget. P4 first
 * verifies that compact residuals and one materialized output pair fit, then caps that annulus's OpenMP worker count
 * so its compact storage plus the estimated concurrent workspaces remain inside the budget. A lower
 * `OMP_NUM_THREADS` value is always respected. The selected and pre-limit worker counts and the per-worker estimate
 * are printed before regression and recorded in the FITS header. If Linux memory discovery is unavailable, P4 emits
 * a warning and retains the OpenMP maximum. Set `p4.memoryFraction=0` to request that behavior explicitly.
 * Frozen-model runs add the retained local response, prepared template, one output-mode field, coordinate/index
 * maps, and bounded per-worker frame-stamp scratch to these checks. Filtering additionally accounts for its four
 * full-frame output cubes. Final reconstruction independently lowers its worker count when necessary and never
 * increases the `OMP_NUM_THREADS` limit.
 *
 * During geometry construction, `p4Reduce` reports each annulus together with its search-pixel and retained predictor
 * counts. During regression, a rate-limited `std::cerr` status line combines the active annulus's completed/total
 * pixels, measured seconds per pixel, and local ETA with cumulative completed/total pixels
 * across all annuli. Each annulus ends with an exact newline-delimited completion record, so redirected logs remain
 * readable as well as interactive terminal output.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_rotated_baseline Rotated mode, predictor baselines, and final ADI processing
 *
 * Rotated regression fits only temporal fluctuations, then applies those coefficients to the complete
 * post-preprocessing predictor values. It can therefore subtract a predictor-derived static stellar-halo or
 * background level even though that level did not affect the fit, but the centered objective neither requires nor
 * validates that subtraction. The AF Lep/NACO acceptance comparison found that the applied predictor DC removed
 * essentially none of the target-only sky baseline. Two inherited, optional detector-frame preprocessing choices
 * alter this baseline convention before rotated regression:
 *
 * - `preProcess.subradprof=true` subtracts a median radial profile independently from each detector image; and
 * - `preProcess.meanSubMethod=medianImage` subtracts the temporal median detector-frame PSF.
 *
 * They may be combined in that order. Neither is forced by rotated mode: radial-profile subtraction can bias
 * localized sources, while a median PSF can self-subtract a companion when the number of frames or field rotation is
 * inadequate. Because predictor DC levels now enter the science residual, injected-source throughput and residual
 * baselines should be checked for the selected preprocessing. An already preprocessed sequence may use
 * `preProcess.skip=true` only when its preprocessing provenance is known.
 * Neither preprocessing option resolves the mathematical ambiguity: detector-frame temporal averaging does not in
 * general zero the mean of a fixed-sky time series after frame-dependent rotation. Removing a fitted sky-frame
 * intercept would also remove a stationary companion. A scientifically accepted rotated reduction therefore needs
 * an independently justified stellar-baseline model or prior; none is currently implemented.
 *
 * `adi.postMedSub=true` remains rejected with rotated regression because it would impose a second, separate sky-fixed
 * baseline subtraction after the predictor policy and confound its throughput contract. Rotated residuals carry an
 * explicit sky-frame state into final processing, so they are never derotated again. `combine.noDerotate` controls
 * only detector-frame residuals and is irrelevant to rotated mode; its default value cannot cause a double
 * derotation.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_example Example configuration
 *
 * This compact example follows the public AF Lep/NACO prototype science geometry and adds a `[3.5,6)` calculation
 * annulus for the current inner-boundary support experiment. Paths remain for the user to select. A checked
 * comparison configuration is provided at `examples/p4Reduce_afLepNaco_prototype.conf` and installed under
 * `share/hcireduce/examples`. It uses the prototype's input path, `p4.rankTolerance=0`, and the sequential `finim_`
 * output prefix. With `exactFName=false`, `output.fileName` is a literal prefix rather than a format string, so this
 * produces `finim_0000.fits` and then the next unused four-digit name.
 *
 * \code{.ini}
 * mode=normal
 *
 * [input]
 * directory=/path/to/preprocessed
 * prefix=pp_
 * extension=.fits
 * dateKeyword=
 * angleKeyword=POSANG
 * angleScale=1
 * angleConstant=1.6
 *
 * [geom]
 * minRadius=3.5,6,8,10,12,14,16,18
 * maxRadius=6,8,10,12,14,16,18,20
 *
 * [p4]
 * regressionFrame=detector
 * numberImages=1
 * modeFractions=0.01,0.03,0.05,0.07,0.09,0.11,0.13,0.15,0.17,0.19,0.21,0.23,0.25,0.27,0.29
 * orDeltaRadiusInner=8
 * orDeltaRadiusOuter=30
 * orArcHalfWidth=32
 * orMaxHalfAngle=90
 * psfRadius=1.5
 * exclusionPolicy=sampleCenter
 * exclusionRadiusBuffer=0.5
 * rankTolerance=1e-12
 * memoryFraction=0.8
 * writeDiagnostics=false
 * diagnosticDirectory=./p4-diagnostics
 *
 * [preProcess]
 * skip=true
 *
 * [combine]
 * method=sigmaMean
 * sigmaThreshold=5
 * minGoodFract=0
 * noDerotate=false
 *
 * [output]
 * directory=./p4-output
 * fileName=finim_
 * exactFName=false
 * outputPSFSub=false
 * \endcode
 *
 * The comparison configuration does not promise bitwise-identical output. The prototype used float/CUDA PCA and
 * zero-filled unsupported pixels, whereas p4Reduce uses all-double CPU PCA and propagates authoritative validity
 * through derotation and combination. The prototype also wrote an unused `psf_mean.fits` sidecar that has no effect
 * on the reduction and is not reproduced by p4Reduce.
 *
 * For a new dataset, leave `preProcess.skip=false` and configure inherited preprocessing explicitly. Diagnostics do
 * not change the numerical result, but the ownership and region-summary products are useful when validating new
 * geometry. `p4Validity_*.fits` records residual validity immediately after local fitting. It is in detector
 * coordinates for detector regression and in the final sky coordinates for rotated regression. Final products record
 * the selection as `HIERARCH P4 FRAME = 'detector'` or `HIERARCH P4 FRAME = 'rotated'`. The documented definition of
 * rotated regression includes centered coefficient fitting and application to the uncentered predictors.
 *
 * \section p4reduce_dictionary Configuration reference
 *
 * The \ref p4_configuration_dictionary "p4Reduce configuration dictionary" gives the command-line spelling,
 * configuration-file form, accepted values, and detailed scientific effect of every P4 setting. The shared
 * \ref common_configuration_dictionary "common dictionary" covers input, preprocessing, ADI, combination, and output.
 *
 * @}
 */
